# compose -- how many answers in each level have to run MORE THAN ONE command.
#
# the curriculum taught every command one at a time and then, almost nowhere,
# asked anyone to put two of them together. measured 2026-09-20, before this
# file existed: of thirty levels, nineteen had ZERO answers with a pipe in
# them, and five more had exactly one. the gauntlet was all composition and
# the gauntlet is locked until every level is held -- so the skill the whole
# system exists to build was the one thing the levels never asked for.
#
# that is the kind of gap that comes back. a level gets rewritten, a card gets
# cut for length, and nothing notices, because no number was watching. this is
# the number.
#
# FORMAT: one line per level.
#   <level-id>  <floor>    at least <floor> answers must invoke 2+ commands
#   <level-id>  -  reason  the level is exempt, and why
#
# the floor is a RATCHET: it is what the level has now, so the count can only
# be raised deliberately. lowering a floor is a decision someone has to make
# in this file, in a diff, with the reason next to it -- never something that
# happens because a card was edited. `learn compose -v` prints the measured
# count beside each floor, which is how you raise one honestly.
#
# "2+ commands" is answer_cmds' reading of the first listed answer: a pipe, a
# && , a ; or a $(...) all count, and a command name inside a quoted awk or
# sed program does not. one command with many flags is one command.
#
# the closed reason set for an exemption:
#   grammar      -- the level teaches shell syntax, not a tool. a pipe there
#                   would be scenery: the lesson is the construct itself.
#   single-tool  -- the level is one interactive program end to end, and
#                   composing it with anything is a different lesson.
#   foundation   -- below the level that introduces the pipe. nothing here
#                   CAN compose yet; 04-redirection is where | is declared.
#
# a level in neither column fails the build. a new level has to say which it
# is, which is the whole point: the question gets asked once, on the commit
# that adds it, instead of never.
